# Testing approach
- Prefer a simple pyramid: unit tests first, then integration tests for important backend flows, then minimal E2E for a few critical user journeys.
- Match the level of testing to the feature. Do not force every change into every test layer.
- Prioritize tests for helpers, calculations, formatting/parsing utilities, indicators, strategy logic, and other domain behavior.
- Do not default to UI rendering tests unless the component contains meaningful business behavior that cannot be tested more directly.

# Rules
- Move reusable calculations and decision-making logic out of UI files into helpers or services so it can be tested without rendering.
- New helper or calculation-heavy code should usually include unit tests.
- Important backend behavior should usually have at least one integration test.
- ML or strategy logic should include at least one behavior or invariant-focused test.
- E2E should be reserved for a small set of high-value flows, not broad page coverage.
- Treat flaky tests as bugs to fix, not noise to ignore.

# Pipeline
- The quality pipeline should run lint, typecheck, and unit tests on every PR or push to protected branches.
- Add heavier integration or E2E stages only when the corresponding product surface becomes stable enough to justify them.
